<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Khadas Edge1 (Rockchip RK3399) - Android TV 14 local manifest.

  This is a repo *local manifest* overlay. It is applied on top of the upstream
  AOSP 14 platform manifest, NOT used standalone:

    repo init -u https://android.googlesource.com/platform/manifest \
              -b ${AOSP_TAG}
    mkdir -p .repo/local_manifests
    cp manifests/khadas_edge_tv14.xml .repo/local_manifests/
    repo sync -c -j$(nproc)

  Rationale for the overlay approach: the legacy khadas-edge-Qt manifest forked
  all 737 AOSP 10 projects into github.com/khadas. Carrying a full fork forward
  to 14 would mean re-forking the entire platform and re-applying every AOSP
  security patch by hand. Instead we track upstream AOSP 14 and fork only the
  Rockchip/Khadas projects that actually contain board support.

  Revisions below are the exact SHAs the khadas-edge-Qt (Android 10) manifest
  pinned. They are the *starting point* of the forward-port, not Android 14
  ready. See docs/PORTING.md for the per-project migration state.
-->
<manifest>

  <remote name="khadas" fetch="https://github.com/khadas/" />

  <!-- ==================================================================
       Removals: Pixel kernel prebuilts.

       These are gigabytes of prebuilt kernel binaries for Pixel phones and have
       nothing to do with an RK3399 TV box. They are dropped for three reasons:
       the disk they save, the sync time, and because they are LFS-backed -
       device/google/bluejay-kernel is what deterministically failed checkout
       here before git-lfs was installed.

       Safe to remove: nothing in an edge1_tv build references another device's
       kernel prebuilt. Names taken verbatim from the AOSP 14 manifest at
       android-14.0.0_r75.

       Not removed, though also unused: external/deqp, external/deqp-deps/*,
       packages/apps/Browser2, prebuilts/qemu-kernel. They are small, and
       dropping a project some other Android.bp might reference risks a Soong
       analysis error that is harder to diagnose than the disk is worth.
       ================================================================== -->
  <remove-project name="device/google/akita-kernel" />
  <remove-project name="device/google/bluejay-kernel" />
  <remove-project name="device/google/coral-kernel" />
  <remove-project name="device/google/felix-kernel" />
  <remove-project name="device/google/lynx-kernel" />
  <remove-project name="device/google/pantah-kernel" />
  <remove-project name="device/google/raviole-kernel" />
  <remove-project name="device/google/redbull-kernel" />
  <remove-project name="device/google/shusky-kernel" />
  <remove-project name="device/google/sunfish-kernel" />
  <remove-project name="device/google/tangorpro-kernel" />
  <!-- ==================================================================
       Kernel: mainline 6.12 LTS, not the Khadas 4.19 BSP.

       This is the pivot the rest of the tree follows. The BSP kernel had the
       Rockchip drivers but every userspace piece that talked to them was
       Android-10 vintage and had to be forward-ported: gralloc, the Mali blob's
       gralloc integration, MPP, the display HAL. Mainline inverts that trade:
       the drivers are upstream and AOSP already ships userspace for them.

         GPU      drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost  <- external/mesa3d, libmesa_pipe_panfrost
         Display  drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip  <- external/drm_hwcomposer + minigbm
         Decode   staging/media/rkvdec      <- external/v4l2_codec2  (H.264 only)
         Wi-Fi    brcmfmac over SDIO        <- AOSP wpa_supplicant
         Audio    simple-audio-card + HDMI  <- AOSP AIDL audio HAL over ALSA

       No proprietary blob is required on this path, and nothing in
       hardware/rockchip or vendor/rockchip is used - which is why they are gone
       from this manifest.

       6.12.111 is the newest 6.12 LTS point release. The series matters: the
       Khadas Edge already runs 6.12 in this owner's OpenWrt build
       (github.com/220242/khadas_edge-openwrt, Linux 6.12.94, mainline U-Boot +
       TF-A), so the board is proven on this kernel with HDMI, eMMC, Ethernet and
       Wi-Fi before Android is added on top.

       Board DTS: arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-khadas-edge.dts
                  (rk3399-khadas-edge-v.dts and -captain.dts are the other two
                  carriers; build/build-kernel.sh takes the name as a variable.)
       Defconfig: arch/arm64/configs/defconfig plus
                  device/khadas/edge/kernel/edge1_mainline.config

       Path is kernel/mainline, not "kernel": a project at path "kernel" would
       nest the 28 upstream kernel/* projects inside itself and repo rejects
       overlapping paths.

       clone-depth="1": the full linux history is about 5 GiB and nothing here
       needs it. A tag plus depth 1 is roughly 250 MiB.
       ================================================================== -->
  <remote name="linux-stable" fetch="https://github.com/gregkh/" />
  <project name="linux" path="kernel/mainline" remote="linux-stable"
           revision="refs/tags/v6.12.111" clone-depth="1" />

  <!-- ==================================================================
       U-Boot: mainline, for a self-booting SD card.

       The board is flashed by writing one whole-disk image to an SD card, so the
       card has to carry its own bootloader - the RK3399 BootROM looks at the SD
       card before the eMMC, which is why the eMMC install is left untouched and a
       card can simply be removed to get the board back.

       khadas-edge-v-rk3399_defconfig is upstream and targets exactly this carrier
       (CONFIG_DEFAULT_DEVICE_TREE="rockchip/rk3399-khadas-edge-v",
       CONFIG_BAUDRATE=1500000, which is the console this tree's kernel command line
       names). What it does not enable is Android boot image support, so
       build/build-uboot.sh merges a fragment for that; see the script for the boot
       sequence, which is U-Boot's own documented one from
       doc/android/boot-image.rst.

       rk3399 needs BL31 and nothing else from rkbin: U-Boot's own TPL does DDR init
       on this SoC (CONFIG_TPL=y with CONFIG_RAM_ROCKCHIP_LPDDR4=y in the defconfig),
       so no ROCKCHIP_TPL blob is involved. doc/board/rockchip/rockchip.rst:279-285
       is the recipe.

       v2026.07 is a release tag rather than master. clone-depth="1" for the same
       reason as the kernel - the full history is not needed to build a tag.
       ================================================================== -->
  <remote name="u-boot" fetch="https://github.com/u-boot/" />
  <project name="u-boot" path="bootloader/u-boot" remote="u-boot"
           revision="refs/tags/v2026.07" clone-depth="1" />

  <!-- ==================================================================
       Rockchip blobs that remain.

       rkbin carries the two things no RK3399 bootloader can be built without,
       whoever builds it: the DDR init blob and rk3399_bl31.elf (TF-A). Mainline
       U-Boot consumes both, which is exactly what the OpenWrt build does.

       Gone from this manifest, with the reason:

         u-boot          the BSP fork, for Rockchip's update.img/maskrom flow.
                         Path B boots with mainline U-Boot, which this board
                         already runs.
         RKTools         afptool/rkImageMaker, the update.img packer. Same reason.
         hardware/rockchip/{libgralloc,hwcomposer,librga,audio,libhwjpeg,
                            omx_il,librkvpu,liblights,power,sensor,
                            libmemtrack,hdmicec,camera}
                         every one of these is the Android-10 HIDL implementation
                         that Path B replaces with AOSP code. Syncing them would
                         be syncing a fork of a fork that nothing builds.
         vendor/rockchip/{common,hardware}
                         the proprietary blobs, chiefly libGLES_mali. panfrost
                         plus Mesa is the whole point of this path; keeping the
                         blob around invites someone to wire it back in against
                         a gralloc it cannot talk to.

       They are all in this file's git history if the BSP path is ever revisited.
       ================================================================== -->
  <project name="android_rkbin" path="rkbin" remote="khadas"
           revision="664ccee1d4ada924848b80b2912d48c7fb30049c" clone-depth="1" />

</manifest>
